CAT(1)                         P32W Unix                          CAT(1)

NAME
       cat - concatenate and print files

SYNOPSIS
       cat [-nu] [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
       cat reads each file in turn and writes it to the standard
       output. With no file, or for an argument of -, it reads the
       standard input.

       It copies bytes unchanged, 1024 at a time, so it serves
       equally for text and binary files. A directory is refused
       ("cat: d: is a directory").

OPTIONS
       -n     number the lines: each line after its number (right
              aligned in six places) and a tab. The numbering runs
              on through all the files.
       -u     accepted and ignored: cat never buffers its output.
       --     ends the options, for a file that starts with -.

EXAMPLES
       cat notes.txt                 show a file
       cat part1 part2 > whole       join two files
       cat -n prog.c                 with line numbers
       cat > memo                    type a file; end it with ^D

EXIT STATUS
       0 if every file was copied; 1 if a file could not be opened
       or read (cat says why and goes on with the next) or a write
       failed; 2 for an unknown option.

SEE ALSO
       more(1), head(1), tail(1), od(1), tee(1), grep(1)

BUGS
       There are no -s, -v, -b or -e options.
